1
00:01:08,368 --> 00:01:11,202
Bad news.

2
00:01:11,371 --> 00:01:13,931
That's what I've got.

3
00:01:14,608 --> 00:01:17,669
Bad news.

4
00:01:21,381 --> 00:01:23,282
There is no will.

5
00:01:28,589 --> 00:01:36,589
Or if there is one, no
one has found it yet.

6
00:01:39,766 --> 00:01:45,034
I called you here
for another reason.

7
00:01:45,505 --> 00:01:52,639
A little discussion
about murder.

8
00:01:54,381 --> 00:01:55,849
Marshall wasn't murdered.

9
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:57,975
We all saw the body.

10
00:01:58,151 --> 00:02:01,144
He was ripped apart by
some hideous creature.

11
00:02:01,321 --> 00:02:03,256
The hounds.

12
00:02:03,423 --> 00:02:07,258
Oh, my God, it
must be the hounds.

13
00:02:07,427 --> 00:02:09,487
Superstitious rot.

14
00:02:09,663 --> 00:02:13,156
You wouldn't say that if you'd
seen what was left of him.

15
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:14,961
It was...

16
00:02:17,671 --> 00:02:19,162
It was...

17
00:02:23,176 --> 00:02:27,511
It was... "Murder, Miss Isabel."

18
00:02:29,816 --> 00:02:30,840
What?

19
00:02:31,018 --> 00:02:32,498
Murder, Miss Isabel.

20
00:02:32,653 --> 00:02:34,121
Murder, Miss Isabel.

21
00:02:34,287 --> 00:02:36,882
That's what it was.

22
00:02:37,057 --> 00:02:40,789
And while I did
not find the will,

23
00:02:41,128 --> 00:02:43,893
I did find...

24
00:02:48,135 --> 00:02:50,604
- Oh, this. Oh, I'm sorry.
- Aah!

25
00:02:51,972 --> 00:02:53,463
I'm sorry about the knife.

26
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:55,302
It got caught on a
thread or something.

27
00:02:55,475 --> 00:02:59,139
All right, all right, all right.

28
00:02:59,312 --> 00:03:01,440
Let's take five, everyone.

29
00:03:03,250 --> 00:03:06,049
Ben, a word with you, please.

30
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:08,752
I'm sorry about the knife.

31
00:03:08,922 --> 00:03:12,290
Either I can't get it out of my
pocket or hang on to it once I do.

32
00:03:12,459 --> 00:03:13,984
But I'll keep working on it.

33
00:03:14,161 --> 00:03:16,289
Good. I'm sure Ms.
Dryden will appreciate that.

34
00:03:16,463 --> 00:03:17,487
Yeah. Uh-huh.

35
00:03:17,664 --> 00:03:23,262
Listen, my friend, your delivery
in this scene seems a little stilted.

36
00:03:23,437 --> 00:03:25,167
Uh-huh. Mm-hm.

37
00:03:25,338 --> 00:03:27,864
- I mean, a little too stilted.
- Oh.

38
00:03:28,041 --> 00:03:29,321
See, you're playing a detective.

39
00:03:29,476 --> 00:03:36,007
But he's a very caring
detective with deep emotions.

40
00:03:37,517 --> 00:03:39,247
You know what I mean?

41
00:03:40,487 --> 00:03:44,652
Oh, yeah, yeah, he's a
detective with, uh, deep emotions.

42
00:03:44,825 --> 00:03:49,559
Yeah, I want you to reach down
inside yourself, into your very soul

43
00:03:49,730 --> 00:03:55,692
and deliver those emotions,
bare yourself to the audience.

44
00:03:55,869 --> 00:03:58,429
Now, this may be community
theater, but it's theater nonetheless.

45
00:03:58,605 --> 00:04:01,473
- So I want passion.
- Passion, right.

46
00:04:01,641 --> 00:04:04,839
Remember, your character
is a vestige of the Old South,

47
00:04:05,011 --> 00:04:07,412
a gentleman of
culture and breeding.

48
00:04:07,581 --> 00:04:09,948
So when you reach
down inside yourself

49
00:04:10,117 --> 00:04:14,714
and deliver those
painful emotions, think...

50
00:04:15,288 --> 00:04:17,985
Think Orson Welles.

51
00:04:18,158 --> 00:04:19,217
Orson Welles. Yeah.

52
00:04:19,392 --> 00:04:21,554
Think someone larger than life.

53
00:04:21,728 --> 00:04:22,821
Larger than life.

54
00:04:22,996 --> 00:04:30,996
A detective who stalks his
prey with civility, patois, panache.

55
00:04:31,671 --> 00:04:33,469
A detective with panache.

56
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:36,610
Now, I realize that neither you nor
anyone else here is a professional,

57
00:04:36,777 --> 00:04:40,646
but the audience that's going
to be sitting out there next week

58
00:04:40,814 --> 00:04:45,309
is still going to expect
to be, nay, want to be,

59
00:04:45,485 --> 00:04:47,351
transported to another realm.

60
00:04:47,521 --> 00:04:49,217
Yeah.

61
00:04:49,389 --> 00:04:52,791
And you can do it,
Ben. We can do it.

62
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:56,728
- But you have to think reality.
- Reality. Reality.

63
00:04:56,897 --> 00:05:00,061
- Reality.
- Reality.

64
00:05:00,233 --> 00:05:01,292
- Good.
- Yeah.

65
00:05:01,468 --> 00:05:03,460
- Let's try it again.
- Okay.

66
00:05:04,838 --> 00:05:05,897
He's terrible.

67
00:05:06,072 --> 00:05:08,234
I've seen kumquats
with more talent.

68
00:05:08,408 --> 00:05:10,502
Give him all the energy
you can. Help him, Jane.

69
00:05:10,677 --> 00:05:13,670
- Help me, help the show.
- I will try.

70
00:05:13,847 --> 00:05:16,476
- All right. Same scene from the top.
- Right.

71
00:05:17,851 --> 00:05:20,047
- Places, everyone.
- From the top.

72
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:22,553
Places, everyone.

73
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:34,624
Billy.

74
00:05:34,801 --> 00:05:38,294
You're supposed to be coming in out of
the rain. It'll help you get in character.

75
00:05:43,443 --> 00:05:45,378
Knock, please, Billy.

76
00:06:04,231 --> 00:06:05,255
Line.

77
00:06:05,432 --> 00:06:09,369
Everyone is gathered in the
drawing room, as you requested.

78
00:06:10,136 --> 00:06:13,300
Everyone is gathered in the
drawing room, as you requested.

79
00:06:14,674 --> 00:06:17,667
Oops. Sorry, Doug, I gotta
take this. It's my broker.

80
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:21,375
What is going on?

81
00:06:21,548 --> 00:06:23,949
We are rehearsing a
play, for heaven's sake.

82
00:06:24,117 --> 00:06:26,177
Cliff has one line.
He can't remember it.

83
00:06:26,353 --> 00:06:27,981
Ben keeps dropping his knife.

84
00:06:28,154 --> 00:06:31,249
Now Carl has to
talk to his broker.

85
00:06:32,058 --> 00:06:34,357
All right. Let's
break for lunch.

86
00:06:35,128 --> 00:06:39,896
I want everyone to take care
of their personal transactions

87
00:06:40,066 --> 00:06:42,900
and be back here
no later than 1:30.

88
00:06:47,274 --> 00:06:48,867
- You got a minute?
- For what?

89
00:06:49,042 --> 00:06:51,102
You know what. We're one
week away from opening.

90
00:06:51,278 --> 00:06:53,270
None of the scenes
work, not one.

91
00:06:53,446 --> 00:06:55,415
Don't look at me.

92
00:06:58,285 --> 00:07:04,714
If you could just rewrite them so
that Ben Matlock has fewer lines.

93
00:07:04,891 --> 00:07:07,326
I told you not to
give him the part.

94
00:07:08,728 --> 00:07:11,323
Beth, I'm begging you.

95
00:07:12,232 --> 00:07:17,227
Doug, if you take lines away
from Hamlet, then it's not <i>Hamlet.</i>

96
00:07:17,404 --> 00:07:18,963
The play hangs on his role.

97
00:07:24,644 --> 00:07:26,476
What am I gonna do?

98
00:07:27,180 --> 00:07:28,273
Isn't Billy coming?

99
00:07:28,448 --> 00:07:31,384
No. He's gonna stay
and work on some lights.

100
00:07:31,551 --> 00:07:33,631
He's really got the
theater bug, doesn't he?

101
00:07:33,787 --> 00:07:34,811
Oh, yeah.

102
00:07:34,988 --> 00:07:37,457
He's not the only
one, is he, Dad?

103
00:07:38,558 --> 00:07:41,722
I think I'm really
getting there now.

104
00:07:41,895 --> 00:07:46,595
I feel like an actor, like I'm
bringing things up from my soul.

105
00:07:46,766 --> 00:07:49,702
Oh, see, that's
Doug. He's a genius.

106
00:07:49,869 --> 00:07:52,100
Yeah, he's good. He's good.

107
00:07:52,272 --> 00:07:54,112
But you know what else it's like?
- Hmm?

108
00:07:54,274 --> 00:08:00,180
It's like being a lawyer is not all
that different from being an actor.

109
00:08:00,347 --> 00:08:02,646
You're up there in
front of an audience.

110
00:08:02,816 --> 00:08:05,285
You know what you wanna say.

111
00:08:05,452 --> 00:08:10,447
But you also gotta know
when to say it and how to say it.

112
00:08:11,791 --> 00:08:13,760
You know that speech
you have in the second act

113
00:08:13,927 --> 00:08:16,658
about how it was
being Marshall's nurse?

114
00:08:17,564 --> 00:08:23,196
You mean: Being his nurse
wasn't a job, it was a privilege.

115
00:08:23,370 --> 00:08:26,169
And none of you will ever
know how much I'll miss him.

116
00:08:26,339 --> 00:08:29,537
You see how you're
emphasizing the word "ever"?

117
00:08:29,709 --> 00:08:32,406
- Yeah.
- Try it this way.

118
00:08:34,981 --> 00:08:39,009
It was a privilege. And
none of you will ever

119
00:08:39,185 --> 00:08:41,780
know how much I'll miss him.

120
00:08:43,189 --> 00:08:45,158
You want me to
emphasize the word "know"?

121
00:08:45,325 --> 00:08:47,021
Exactly. See what I mean?

122
00:08:47,193 --> 00:08:51,494
Just one little inflection
makes all the difference, heh.

123
00:08:51,664 --> 00:08:54,099
Say it that way from now on.

124
00:08:57,070 --> 00:09:00,598
I'd better check it out with
Doug just to make sure.

125
00:09:01,941 --> 00:09:03,821
What about me? I know
I only have this one line,

126
00:09:03,977 --> 00:09:06,674
but can you give me any tips?

127
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:13,582
Well, Cliff, just
try to remember it.

128
00:09:21,661 --> 00:09:24,221
Tell Jeff I looked
over the files last night.

129
00:09:24,397 --> 00:09:26,559
He's to go ahead and
approve the Stevenson loan.

130
00:09:26,733 --> 00:09:29,362
But under no circumstances is
he to move on the Conklin loan

131
00:09:29,536 --> 00:09:31,562
until he and I talk.

132
00:09:31,738 --> 00:09:33,764
No, it'll have to be tomorrow.

133
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:35,966
I'll be here rehearsing
until at least 10.

134
00:09:38,912 --> 00:09:42,872
Can't. Gotta go. Put it
onto my desk. Okay. Bye.

135
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:49,348
Doug.

136
00:09:52,659 --> 00:09:54,025
Hi.

137
00:09:54,194 --> 00:09:55,526
Wanna go get some lunch?

138
00:09:55,695 --> 00:09:57,061
No. I, uh, brought a sandwich.

139
00:09:57,230 --> 00:09:59,756
I thought I'd stay in my office
and get some paperwork done.

140
00:10:02,235 --> 00:10:05,899
Couldn't help but notice how,
um, chummy you seem to be getting

141
00:10:06,072 --> 00:10:09,133
with Jane Dryden these days.

142
00:10:09,309 --> 00:10:10,607
I am the director.

143
00:10:10,777 --> 00:10:14,077
- I was just giving her direction.
- Oh, heh.

144
00:10:15,215 --> 00:10:18,447
Does your wife know how
hands-on you tend to be

145
00:10:18,618 --> 00:10:21,611
when directing beautiful women?

146
00:10:22,188 --> 00:10:24,180
Glenda, don't.

147
00:10:24,357 --> 00:10:26,383
Let's have dinner. - I can't.

148
00:10:26,559 --> 00:10:29,893
Sure, you can. Go to my place.

149
00:10:30,063 --> 00:10:32,623
You know where that
is by now, don't you?

150
00:10:33,533 --> 00:10:35,502
This has got to stop.

151
00:10:35,668 --> 00:10:37,193
You're the one who started it.

152
00:10:38,138 --> 00:10:39,629
Six o'clock.

153
00:10:39,806 --> 00:10:41,138
Tell your wife you
won't be home.

154
00:10:42,008 --> 00:10:44,534
Or would you like for me to?

155
00:10:47,847 --> 00:10:49,748
Six o'clock.

156
00:10:56,890 --> 00:11:00,292
Then opened the window,

157
00:11:04,130 --> 00:11:05,758
looked out,

158
00:11:06,266 --> 00:11:10,761
dropped the knife
down the rain spout,

159
00:11:10,937 --> 00:11:12,633
knowing

160
00:11:13,573 --> 00:11:20,810
that even if someone
like me were to find it,

161
00:11:20,980 --> 00:11:24,815
the rain would
wash it clean of blood

162
00:11:26,252 --> 00:11:27,618
And fingerprints.

163
00:11:27,787 --> 00:11:30,814
You are wrong, Mr. Angeline.

164
00:11:31,191 --> 00:11:33,092
I saw the body.

165
00:11:33,259 --> 00:11:37,492
And I cannot believe,
no, I refuse to believe,

166
00:11:37,664 --> 00:11:40,964
that one human being
could do that to another.

167
00:11:41,134 --> 00:11:42,864
It was the hounds.

168
00:11:43,036 --> 00:11:44,800
I knew it.

169
00:11:44,971 --> 00:11:46,496
They've come back.

170
00:11:46,673 --> 00:11:48,335
And where are they now, Vanessa?

171
00:11:48,508 --> 00:11:50,443
Out in the yard?

172
00:11:50,610 --> 00:11:55,605
Actually, speaking
of whereabouts...

173
00:12:03,323 --> 00:12:06,259
Aw, Ben, for God's sake.

174
00:12:06,426 --> 00:12:07,985
We're one week
away from opening.

175
00:12:08,161 --> 00:12:10,790
You've yet to get through a
scene without fluffing your lines.

176
00:12:10,964 --> 00:12:12,990
Glenda's winking at me.

177
00:12:13,166 --> 00:12:15,761
I have something
behind my contact lens.

178
00:12:15,935 --> 00:12:18,404
Well, don't just stand
there. Stay in character.

179
00:12:18,571 --> 00:12:21,769
Improvise, turn away,
pace, use your brain.

180
00:12:21,941 --> 00:12:24,381
You've distracted him. You've
broken everyone's concentration.

181
00:12:24,510 --> 00:12:26,536
You should know
better than that.

182
00:12:32,752 --> 00:12:35,779
Are you sure you want
to speak to me like that?

183
00:12:41,060 --> 00:12:42,824
We'll take it again.

184
00:12:44,530 --> 00:12:50,959
I love the clean
fragrance in the air

185
00:12:55,341 --> 00:13:03,341
after the rain has stopped
and justice has been done.

186
00:13:09,989 --> 00:13:11,855
He's horrible.

187
00:13:12,025 --> 00:13:15,325
I told you that
during auditions.

188
00:13:15,495 --> 00:13:18,431
But did you listen? No.

189
00:13:19,532 --> 00:13:24,197
I thought we should have a name,
someone people would come to see.

190
00:13:25,171 --> 00:13:27,766
Curtain, please, Billy.

191
00:13:29,609 --> 00:13:31,635
All right, let's
break for dinner.

192
00:13:31,811 --> 00:13:32,835
Back at 7 sharp.

193
00:13:33,012 --> 00:13:35,504
We'll run the whole
thing through from the top.

194
00:13:35,682 --> 00:13:42,350
Oh, Ben, a word with you,
please, out in the lobby.

195
00:13:45,325 --> 00:13:47,521
- Ahh.
- That last scene felt good.

196
00:13:47,694 --> 00:13:49,334
That's what I wanted
to talk to you about.

197
00:13:49,462 --> 00:13:52,432
I'm getting the hang of this,
Doug, a handle on my character.

198
00:13:52,598 --> 00:13:54,760
I'm reaching down into
my soul, like you said.

199
00:13:54,934 --> 00:13:58,302
I really thought I played the
truth of the moment just now.

200
00:13:58,471 --> 00:14:00,591
- Ben, I've got to replace you.
- Truth of the moment.

201
00:14:00,740 --> 00:14:03,369
If actors would just play the...

202
00:14:07,714 --> 00:14:08,738
How's that?

203
00:14:09,682 --> 00:14:12,379
I've got to replace you.

204
00:14:15,521 --> 00:14:16,784
You're firing me?

205
00:14:17,290 --> 00:14:19,657
Ben, I made a mistake.

206
00:14:19,826 --> 00:14:21,886
I never should have
cast you in this role.

207
00:14:22,795 --> 00:14:24,388
But I'm getting
better every day.

208
00:14:24,564 --> 00:14:26,396
No. It's more than just that.

209
00:14:26,566 --> 00:14:30,162
A director has to
consider the entire cast.

210
00:14:30,336 --> 00:14:35,240
If it doesn't mesh, then
changes have to be made.

211
00:14:35,408 --> 00:14:38,310
Well, we're not meshing?

212
00:14:38,478 --> 00:14:39,502
That's right.

213
00:14:39,679 --> 00:14:43,446
Well, how do you know it's
my fault we're not meshing?

214
00:14:43,616 --> 00:14:45,983
Maybe if you fired
somebody else, we'd mesh.

215
00:14:46,152 --> 00:14:48,883
Ben, I'm trying to
be nice about this.

216
00:14:49,055 --> 00:14:50,990
How do you know it's my
fault we're not meshing?

217
00:14:51,157 --> 00:14:53,285
Ben, I thought I could
turn you into an actor.

218
00:14:53,459 --> 00:14:56,395
After all, you've been performing in
front of judges and juries for years.

219
00:14:56,562 --> 00:14:58,087
I was just saying that at lunch.

220
00:14:58,264 --> 00:14:59,732
Ben, I was wrong.

221
00:14:59,899 --> 00:15:03,063
You're not an actor,
never were, never will be.

222
00:15:03,236 --> 00:15:07,332
But you can't find somebody to
replace me at the last minute like this.

223
00:15:08,708 --> 00:15:10,609
In this world, unemployed actors

224
00:15:10,777 --> 00:15:14,373
are only slightly less
numerous than insects.

225
00:15:14,547 --> 00:15:17,107
I'll do your lines
tonight at the rehearsal.

226
00:15:17,283 --> 00:15:19,275
You don't have to come back.

227
00:15:20,620 --> 00:15:22,589
Sorry it didn't work out.

228
00:15:33,099 --> 00:15:37,503
There's a broken heart for
every light on Broadway, Ben.

229
00:15:38,137 --> 00:15:40,038
Keep your chin up.

230
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:42,939
You might get another break.

231
00:15:51,818 --> 00:15:53,787
Okay, boys and
girls, from the top.

232
00:16:22,982 --> 00:16:26,817
Miss Yarnell, you're
supposed to be backstage.

233
00:16:27,753 --> 00:16:30,814
Everybody's waiting for you.

234
00:16:33,759 --> 00:16:35,921
Miss Yarnell.

235
00:16:37,363 --> 00:16:38,763
Yoo-hoo.

236
00:16:47,473 --> 00:16:48,736
Help!

237
00:16:48,908 --> 00:16:50,843
Help, somebody! Help!

238
00:16:57,750 --> 00:16:59,070
You're saying Glenda
was murdered?

239
00:16:59,218 --> 00:17:01,050
Was she ever.

240
00:17:02,154 --> 00:17:03,178
Strangled.

241
00:17:03,356 --> 00:17:04,984
Right in her own dressing room.

242
00:17:05,157 --> 00:17:06,386
I was the one that found her.

243
00:17:07,760 --> 00:17:09,922
Glad I don't do that every day.

244
00:17:10,329 --> 00:17:11,929
Police have any idea who did it?

245
00:17:12,098 --> 00:17:13,122
I don't think so.

246
00:17:13,299 --> 00:17:16,326
They sealed off her dressing room
and questioned us, sent us home.

247
00:17:16,502 --> 00:17:17,993
Forensics will be
in there all night.

248
00:17:18,170 --> 00:17:20,850
I heard the coroner say that Glenda
only had one of her contacts in.

249
00:17:21,007 --> 00:17:22,887
Apparently, the other
one was still in the case.

250
00:17:25,177 --> 00:17:28,045
She was lying in the
middle of the room.

251
00:17:30,316 --> 00:17:32,911
You know, you'd think that if
she was putting in her contacts,

252
00:17:33,085 --> 00:17:35,987
she would have been found over
by the sink, where her lens case was.

253
00:17:36,155 --> 00:17:37,555
Maybe there was a struggle,

254
00:17:37,690 --> 00:17:40,057
and that's how she wound
up in the middle of the room.

255
00:17:40,226 --> 00:17:41,694
Yeah, I guess. I don't know.

256
00:17:41,861 --> 00:17:43,625
Somehow it just
didn't look right to me.

257
00:17:43,996 --> 00:17:47,057
I bet if you'd been there, you
would have thought it was odd.

258
00:17:47,233 --> 00:17:50,761
Unfortunately, I wasn't.

259
00:17:51,804 --> 00:17:52,897
Oh, Dad.

260
00:17:56,609 --> 00:17:58,771
I'm so sorry you got fired.

261
00:17:58,945 --> 00:18:01,608
- Replaced. LEANNE:
Oh, replaced.

262
00:18:01,781 --> 00:18:03,215
Now, don't let
this get you down.

263
00:18:03,382 --> 00:18:07,513
You are still one of the
best lawyers in this country.

264
00:18:08,521 --> 00:18:10,319
Was I really that bad?

265
00:18:10,489 --> 00:18:11,980
You stunk.

266
00:18:12,158 --> 00:18:14,855
Even I could tell that, and
I'm just the stage manager.

267
00:18:23,869 --> 00:18:25,565
Hello?

268
00:18:26,138 --> 00:18:28,835
What? You're kidding.

269
00:18:32,345 --> 00:18:34,177
Well, I don't know.

270
00:18:35,414 --> 00:18:38,816
Okay. Okay. I'll be
there in a few minutes.

271
00:18:38,985 --> 00:18:40,578
Who was that?

272
00:18:41,921 --> 00:18:44,049
Doug Michaels. He's
being held for murder.

273
00:18:44,223 --> 00:18:45,350
Glenda's murder?

274
00:18:45,524 --> 00:18:47,390
Wants me to be his lawyer.

275
00:18:47,560 --> 00:18:49,756
Don't do it, Ben. Whatever
you do, don't take his case.

276
00:18:49,929 --> 00:18:51,795
- Why not?
- Because he did it.

277
00:18:52,198 --> 00:18:53,860
I heard him and
Glenda talking backstage

278
00:18:54,033 --> 00:18:55,968
when I was up on the
ladder fixing the light.

279
00:18:56,135 --> 00:18:58,195
Sounded like they'd been

280
00:18:58,371 --> 00:19:00,806
tiptoeing through the
cabbage patch with each other,

281
00:19:00,973 --> 00:19:03,238
only Doug wanted to
break it off and she didn't.

282
00:19:03,709 --> 00:19:05,234
So he killed her?

283
00:19:05,411 --> 00:19:06,845
I said he did, didn't I?

284
00:19:16,722 --> 00:19:18,782
Were you and Glenda
having an affair?

285
00:19:18,958 --> 00:19:23,293
No. We were not. We just slept
together a few times. That's all.

286
00:19:24,063 --> 00:19:25,258
How many times?

287
00:19:25,431 --> 00:19:26,660
Half a dozen or so.

288
00:19:26,832 --> 00:19:29,893
A few weeks ago, my wife
went away for the weekend.

289
00:19:30,069 --> 00:19:33,562
Glenda asked me to come over
and coach her on some of her scenes.

290
00:19:33,739 --> 00:19:37,141
Well, we had some wine, and
then we had some more wine,

291
00:19:37,309 --> 00:19:40,746
and finally, one
thing led to another.

292
00:19:40,913 --> 00:19:43,883
It was just a moment's
indiscretion to me, but not to Glenda.

293
00:19:44,050 --> 00:19:45,279
She became obsessed.

294
00:19:45,451 --> 00:19:47,784
She wouldn't let go, said if
I didn't continue seeing her,

295
00:19:47,953 --> 00:19:49,922
she'd tell my wife
and ruin my marriage.

296
00:19:50,089 --> 00:19:52,649
I couldn't let that
happen. I love Flora.

297
00:19:52,825 --> 00:19:56,489
- So you cheated on her.
- Precisely.

298
00:19:57,897 --> 00:20:01,163
Ahh, that does have an
odd ring to it, doesn't it?

299
00:20:02,735 --> 00:20:04,363
It gives you motive.

300
00:20:04,537 --> 00:20:07,166
And fibers from your tie
were found on Glenda's neck.

301
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:10,610
My tie was on the desk in my
office, which I never bother to lock.

302
00:20:10,776 --> 00:20:13,245
Anyone could have stolen
it and used it to kill her.

303
00:20:13,412 --> 00:20:15,540
Well, the police
found your tie, Doug,

304
00:20:15,715 --> 00:20:18,651
in the waste basket with
some trash on top of it.

305
00:20:18,818 --> 00:20:21,058
They think you were hiding
it until you left the theater.

306
00:20:23,989 --> 00:20:27,858
This is so Kafkaesque.
I can't believe it.

307
00:20:28,327 --> 00:20:31,058
- Kafka-what?
- Esque.

308
00:20:31,230 --> 00:20:33,665
It's surreal. It's nightmarish.

309
00:20:35,034 --> 00:20:36,798
Who knew about you and Glenda?

310
00:20:36,969 --> 00:20:39,268
I guess everybody in the
cast who saw her slap me

311
00:20:39,438 --> 00:20:41,703
knew something was going on.

312
00:20:42,541 --> 00:20:45,340
Someone in the cast
must have framed me.

313
00:20:45,878 --> 00:20:48,143
That's what you're
thinking, isn't it?

314
00:20:49,515 --> 00:20:51,143
Could be.

315
00:20:51,717 --> 00:20:54,482
Ben, I'm asking you
what you're thinking.

316
00:20:54,653 --> 00:20:58,385
Yeah. It could be somebody
in the cast, I guess.

317
00:20:59,859 --> 00:21:01,327
You're mad at me, aren't you?

318
00:21:01,894 --> 00:21:04,420
- Nope. DOUG: That's
why you're so quiet.

319
00:21:04,830 --> 00:21:07,527
You're sulking just because I
removed you from that silly play.

320
00:21:07,700 --> 00:21:09,430
- I'm not sulking.
- You're sulking.

321
00:21:09,602 --> 00:21:10,762
I know sulking when I see it.

322
00:21:10,903 --> 00:21:13,737
I'm a director, for God's sake. I
see sulking every day of my life.

323
00:21:13,906 --> 00:21:15,431
It's what actors do best.

324
00:21:15,608 --> 00:21:18,339
See? That proves
I'm not sulking.

325
00:21:18,511 --> 00:21:19,843
I'm no actor.

326
00:21:20,579 --> 00:21:22,377
According to you.

327
00:21:23,916 --> 00:21:27,512
Look, I'm sorry if I
hurt your feelings.

328
00:21:28,487 --> 00:21:33,016
I didn't mean to. I was
just being brutally frank.

329
00:21:33,592 --> 00:21:37,029
Please don't hold
that against me.

330
00:21:37,396 --> 00:21:38,420
I need you, Ben.

331
00:21:39,732 --> 00:21:42,725
I could go to prison for
the rest of my life here.

332
00:21:43,169 --> 00:21:44,762
You gotta help me.

333
00:21:46,839 --> 00:21:48,307
Well...

334
00:21:50,509 --> 00:21:52,978
For one thing, I'm expensive.

335
00:21:53,145 --> 00:21:55,671
Fine. I'm rich. What else?

336
00:21:55,848 --> 00:21:58,113
I want my part back.

337
00:21:58,751 --> 00:22:01,186
- Oh.
- In the play.

338
00:22:02,254 --> 00:22:03,779
Uh-huh.

339
00:22:08,594 --> 00:22:11,063
You're thinking it over?

340
00:22:13,065 --> 00:22:15,296
He's thinking it over.

341
00:22:21,740 --> 00:22:24,869
Everyone is gathered in the
drawing room, as you requested.

342
00:22:25,044 --> 00:22:27,980
Everyone is gathered in the
drawing room, as you requested.

343
00:22:28,147 --> 00:22:30,878
- Everyone is gathered in the drawing...
- Cliff, for heaven's sake.

344
00:22:31,050 --> 00:22:33,315
Just trying to get it right,
you know? Everyone is...

345
00:22:33,485 --> 00:22:36,353
The bank president said
Glenda worked here for 11 years,

346
00:22:36,522 --> 00:22:40,289
made it through two mergers,
and got promoted three times.

347
00:22:41,360 --> 00:22:43,829
- As you requested...
- This is odd.

348
00:22:43,996 --> 00:22:45,020
Drawing... What?

349
00:22:45,197 --> 00:22:47,325
Jane Dryden, the woman
who plays Miss Isabel,

350
00:22:47,499 --> 00:22:49,593
- she works here.
- Who?

351
00:22:49,768 --> 00:22:52,465
She's a teller. We
passed her on the way in.

352
00:22:52,638 --> 00:22:55,836
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. Everyone...
- She used to be a loan officer.

353
00:22:56,008 --> 00:22:57,874
Would you look at this?

354
00:22:58,043 --> 00:23:00,842
She was bumped to
a teller last January.

355
00:23:01,013 --> 00:23:04,882
Ho-ho, she took a
50 percent cut in pay.

356
00:23:05,150 --> 00:23:07,449
Wonder why she just didn't quit.

357
00:23:07,620 --> 00:23:10,317
Everyone is gathered in the
drawing room, as you requested.

358
00:23:10,489 --> 00:23:13,687
Everyone is gathered in
the drawing room, as you...

359
00:23:13,859 --> 00:23:17,193
No, everyone is gathered in the
drawing room, as you requested.

360
00:23:20,032 --> 00:23:22,968
I like it here. It's a
nice place to work.

361
00:23:23,135 --> 00:23:25,695
But your salary was
practically cut in half.

362
00:23:25,871 --> 00:23:27,471
Well, the bank was
going through a merger

363
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:29,131
and making
cutbacks right and left.

364
00:23:29,308 --> 00:23:32,608
It was either take a
lower-paying job or be let go.

365
00:23:32,778 --> 00:23:34,508
I wasn't thrilled about
making less money,

366
00:23:34,680 --> 00:23:38,048
but at least I still
have health insurance.

367
00:23:38,217 --> 00:23:40,686
Glenda demoted you, didn't she?

368
00:23:41,654 --> 00:23:45,091
Yes, but Glenda
gave me this job.

369
00:23:45,257 --> 00:23:47,283
She was my friend.

370
00:23:47,459 --> 00:23:50,952
- Thank you, sir. Have a nice day.
<i>- You too.</i>

371
00:23:53,332 --> 00:23:56,200
You know, it never seemed
that way back at the theater.

372
00:23:56,368 --> 00:23:58,368
I mean, I didn't even know
you two worked together,

373
00:23:58,537 --> 00:23:59,835
let alone that you were friends.

374
00:24:00,005 --> 00:24:03,669
Oh. Well, we've known
each other almost eight years.

375
00:24:03,842 --> 00:24:05,868
The play was her idea.

376
00:24:06,045 --> 00:24:07,565
I never would have
done it without her.

377
00:24:07,713 --> 00:24:10,205
<i>- Morning.</i>
- Morning.

378
00:24:10,816 --> 00:24:13,217
Well, we should let you get
back to work. Thanks a lot, Jane.

379
00:24:13,385 --> 00:24:14,717
Sure. Oh, I'll see you tomorrow.

380
00:24:14,887 --> 00:24:15,980
- Bye.
- Bye.

381
00:24:16,155 --> 00:24:18,715
Thanks. Everyone is
gathered in the drawing room...

382
00:24:18,891 --> 00:24:20,723
Did that make sense to you?

383
00:24:20,893 --> 00:24:23,954
Uh, yeah, I guess it did.
I'm trying to learn this line.

384
00:24:24,129 --> 00:24:25,809
Everyone is gathered
in the drawing room...

385
00:24:25,898 --> 00:24:28,060
You know what? I'm gonna
take my dad out to dinner,

386
00:24:28,233 --> 00:24:30,793
because I think he's been so
wrapped up in the stage-manager job,

387
00:24:30,936 --> 00:24:32,564
he hasn't had a
decent meal in weeks.

388
00:24:32,738 --> 00:24:34,263
I bet.

389
00:24:34,440 --> 00:24:35,806
- You wanna come?
- What?

390
00:24:35,975 --> 00:24:38,809
Oh, no, thanks. Dad wants me to
take a look around Glenda's place.

391
00:24:38,978 --> 00:24:41,106
He's home studying his lines.

392
00:24:41,280 --> 00:24:42,873
Oh, good. Good.

393
00:24:43,048 --> 00:24:44,812
Everyone is gathered
in the drawing room...

394
00:25:29,361 --> 00:25:30,522
Oh.

395
00:25:36,568 --> 00:25:37,968
Hello, Beth.

396
00:25:38,537 --> 00:25:40,836
Leanne, what are you doing here?

397
00:25:41,407 --> 00:25:44,275
My dad's decided
to be Doug's lawyer.

398
00:25:44,777 --> 00:25:45,938
So I heard.

399
00:25:46,111 --> 00:25:47,602
Thought I'd look
over Glenda's things,

400
00:25:47,780 --> 00:25:49,874
help him figure out
who really killed her.

401
00:25:50,849 --> 00:25:52,977
The police gave me a
key. How'd you get in?

402
00:25:53,719 --> 00:25:57,156
- Oh, well...
- You broke in?

403
00:25:58,557 --> 00:26:01,425
Glenda borrowed some things
of mine. I just wanted them back.

404
00:26:02,561 --> 00:26:04,086
What kinds of things?

405
00:26:04,930 --> 00:26:07,422
This doesn't concern
you or your father.

406
00:26:07,599 --> 00:26:10,967
Why would Glenda borrow a photo
album from you? Is that a scrapbook?

407
00:26:11,136 --> 00:26:12,627
It's none of your business.

408
00:26:12,805 --> 00:26:16,742
I can call the police and make it
their business. Is that what you want?

409
00:26:37,229 --> 00:26:40,688
Glenda Yarnell was my mother.

410
00:26:51,877 --> 00:26:53,937
No kidding. Her mother.

411
00:26:54,113 --> 00:26:57,277
Said she was adopted 25 years
ago by a couple in New York,

412
00:26:57,449 --> 00:26:58,889
started looking
for her birth mother

413
00:26:59,051 --> 00:27:00,986
almost as soon as
they told her the truth,

414
00:27:01,153 --> 00:27:03,213
and she found Glenda
about a year ago.

415
00:27:03,388 --> 00:27:04,412
She call her?

416
00:27:04,590 --> 00:27:08,118
No. She said she was too afraid of
being rejected to contact her directly.

417
00:27:08,527 --> 00:27:10,723
- What'd she do?
- She's a writer,

418
00:27:10,896 --> 00:27:14,594
and she found out how active
Glenda was in the theater down here.

419
00:27:14,766 --> 00:27:16,735
So she sent the
director one of her plays

420
00:27:16,902 --> 00:27:20,168
and used that as a way to
ease her way into her mom's life.

421
00:27:20,339 --> 00:27:24,538
Had she eased her way
in by the time Glenda died?

422
00:27:24,710 --> 00:27:28,647
No. Beth said that she never
told Glenda who she really was.

423
00:27:28,814 --> 00:27:32,376
- Huh.
- Never worked up the nerve.

424
00:27:33,152 --> 00:27:41,152
Knowing that even if
someone like me were to find it,

425
00:27:41,593 --> 00:27:48,124
the rain would wash away
the blood and the fingerprints.

426
00:27:49,234 --> 00:27:51,135
Maybe she lied.

427
00:27:51,503 --> 00:27:53,303
Maybe she talked to
her and it didn't go well.

428
00:27:53,438 --> 00:27:57,398
Maybe she wound up resenting
Glenda so much that she killed her.

429
00:27:57,576 --> 00:27:59,636
I don't know, Dad. She
was stealing a photo album.

430
00:27:59,811 --> 00:28:02,576
She wanted something to
remember her real mom by.

431
00:28:02,748 --> 00:28:06,116
I don't know why she'd do that
if she was the one that killed her.

432
00:28:06,285 --> 00:28:07,480
Oh.

433
00:28:07,653 --> 00:28:11,852
On the other hand, she said
she was waiting around the theater

434
00:28:12,024 --> 00:28:15,552
just like everybody else on
the night that Glenda died,

435
00:28:15,727 --> 00:28:19,687
and I don't remember seeing
her until after the police arrived.

436
00:28:19,865 --> 00:28:22,225
I'd better check this out with
Cliff, see what he remembers.

437
00:28:22,367 --> 00:28:25,701
Do that tomorrow. Stay
here and run lines with me.

438
00:28:26,238 --> 00:28:28,434
Oh, I'd better check
this out with Cliff, Dad.

439
00:28:34,513 --> 00:28:37,039
As I'm sure most, if
not all, of you know,

440
00:28:37,216 --> 00:28:39,617
Ben Matlock has
agreed to defend me,

441
00:28:39,785 --> 00:28:44,086
and I, in turn, have reconsidered
the hasty decision I made

442
00:28:44,256 --> 00:28:46,953
on the eve of
Glenda's tragic death

443
00:28:47,125 --> 00:28:51,187
and have returned him to
the role of Monroe Angeline.

444
00:28:59,104 --> 00:29:06,238
I'd like to apologize to all of you
for the gigantic mess I've created.

445
00:29:06,411 --> 00:29:10,507
Mostly, I apologize to
my beautiful wife, Flora.

446
00:29:10,682 --> 00:29:12,378
Though I don't deserve it,

447
00:29:12,551 --> 00:29:15,646
she's declared her
intention to stay by my side.

448
00:29:15,821 --> 00:29:18,188
And trouper that she is,
she's also come to our rescue

449
00:29:18,357 --> 00:29:21,725
and will assume
the role of Vanessa.

450
00:29:21,893 --> 00:29:23,759
- I love you, darling.
- Oh.

451
00:29:26,398 --> 00:29:28,867
In any case, opening
night looms ahead.

452
00:29:29,034 --> 00:29:32,266
We'll spend the next few days and
nights rehearsing as never before.

453
00:29:32,437 --> 00:29:35,965
So let's get started. Places,
everyone. From the top.

454
00:29:36,141 --> 00:29:37,165
All right.

455
00:29:38,543 --> 00:29:39,602
From the top.

456
00:29:39,778 --> 00:29:42,418
Everyone is gathered... Everyone
is gathered in the drawing room...

457
00:29:42,481 --> 00:29:44,313
Did you see Jane?
Did you talk to her?

458
00:29:44,483 --> 00:29:45,507
Yeah, yeah. I did.

459
00:29:45,684 --> 00:29:47,884
She said that she didn't
remember seeing Beth that night

460
00:29:48,020 --> 00:29:50,387
until after the police
got here, either.

461
00:29:50,722 --> 00:29:52,350
We might as well
talk to the whole cast

462
00:29:52,524 --> 00:29:55,153
and see if anyone
remembers seeing her.

463
00:29:56,561 --> 00:30:00,225
Do you think we should
confront Beth again right away?

464
00:30:00,565 --> 00:30:02,557
No, Miss Isabel,

465
00:30:02,734 --> 00:30:09,402
I'm afraid human beings are
quite capable of such horrors,

466
00:30:09,574 --> 00:30:14,410
a fact which you know
all too well, don't you?

467
00:30:15,781 --> 00:30:17,477
Never mind.

468
00:30:21,453 --> 00:30:27,950
I love the clean
fragrance in the air...

469
00:30:29,328 --> 00:30:30,728
Ah.

470
00:30:30,896 --> 00:30:38,770
After the rain has stopped
and justice has been done.

471
00:30:44,042 --> 00:30:46,602
I think he's gotten
much better, don't you?

472
00:30:48,046 --> 00:30:50,709
Ahem. Bravo! Bravo!

473
00:30:50,882 --> 00:30:52,942
Curtain, please, Billy.

474
00:30:53,118 --> 00:30:55,019
Excellent, one and all.

475
00:30:55,187 --> 00:30:57,782
Ben, you were superb.

476
00:30:58,924 --> 00:31:02,326
You don't think he said that just
because I'm his lawyer, do you?

477
00:31:03,028 --> 00:31:05,088
No, Dad, heh.

478
00:31:05,764 --> 00:31:08,495
Well, this is it.

479
00:31:08,667 --> 00:31:10,727
Opening night. Ha, ha.

480
00:31:10,902 --> 00:31:12,495
I want everybody
to grab a light dinner,

481
00:31:12,671 --> 00:31:15,505
be back here at 6:30 to get
back into costume and makeup,

482
00:31:15,674 --> 00:31:21,272
7:45 green room,
8:00 curtain up.

483
00:31:21,780 --> 00:31:24,511
Oh, uh, one other thing.

484
00:31:24,683 --> 00:31:27,482
It appears that Glenda's death

485
00:31:27,652 --> 00:31:29,587
has generated a
great deal of national

486
00:31:29,755 --> 00:31:32,691
as well as local interest
in our little production.

487
00:31:32,858 --> 00:31:35,487
Critics from two
weekly news magazines

488
00:31:35,660 --> 00:31:37,380
have indicated they'll
be out there tonight,

489
00:31:37,529 --> 00:31:39,998
as well as reviewers
from the daily papers.

490
00:31:40,165 --> 00:31:42,999
So on your toes.

491
00:31:43,635 --> 00:31:47,367
But most of all, have
fun. Huh? Ha, ha.

492
00:31:52,377 --> 00:31:54,403
How many critics did he
say would be out there?

493
00:31:54,579 --> 00:31:55,842
Just about a dozen or so.

494
00:31:56,014 --> 00:31:57,346
You wanna get a bite?

495
00:31:57,516 --> 00:31:58,609
- Leanne.
- Yeah?

496
00:31:58,784 --> 00:32:00,810
I'm gonna go sign some
checks before dinner.

497
00:32:00,986 --> 00:32:02,946
But before I do, I figured
you'd want to see this.

498
00:32:03,088 --> 00:32:04,112
What's that?

499
00:32:04,289 --> 00:32:05,969
Remember when I told
you Beth wasn't around

500
00:32:06,124 --> 00:32:07,924
- the night Billy found Glenda's body?
- Yeah.

501
00:32:08,093 --> 00:32:09,220
Well, I know where she was.

502
00:32:09,394 --> 00:32:11,124
This reminded me.

503
00:32:11,296 --> 00:32:13,288
- Phone bill?
- For my cellular phone.

504
00:32:13,465 --> 00:32:14,665
I let her borrow it that night

505
00:32:14,833 --> 00:32:17,393
so she could make an
emergency call to New York.

506
00:32:17,569 --> 00:32:21,233
She wanted some privacy, so
she went off in a corner by herself.

507
00:32:22,641 --> 00:32:25,236
See? That's the charge.

508
00:32:25,410 --> 00:32:27,936
She was on the
phone for 27 minutes.

509
00:32:28,113 --> 00:32:30,048
Wonder why she
didn't tell me about that.

510
00:32:30,916 --> 00:32:35,149
I was upset that night.
It just slipped my mind.

511
00:32:35,887 --> 00:32:37,651
I remembered a day or two later.

512
00:32:37,823 --> 00:32:39,621
Why didn't you tell
me about it then?

513
00:32:40,625 --> 00:32:43,254
I guess I just
didn't feel like it.

514
00:32:44,196 --> 00:32:45,289
Huh.

515
00:32:46,598 --> 00:32:49,295
Well, thanks, Carl.

516
00:32:49,468 --> 00:32:52,199
You bet. Break a leg tonight.

517
00:32:52,370 --> 00:32:54,396
Ah, you too.

518
00:32:55,474 --> 00:32:57,409
What do you think of that, Dad?

519
00:32:58,810 --> 00:33:00,335
Dad?

520
00:33:02,380 --> 00:33:03,871
Dad?

521
00:33:09,020 --> 00:33:10,181
- Dad?
- Yeah?

522
00:33:10,355 --> 00:33:13,553
Well, Beth Samuels has an
alibi for the time of the murder.

523
00:33:13,725 --> 00:33:15,525
She's about the only one
around here who does.

524
00:33:15,660 --> 00:33:18,580
I mean, anybody could have slipped
off for five minutes and killed Glenda.

525
00:33:18,730 --> 00:33:21,325
- Even the janitor.
- Yeah.

526
00:33:23,668 --> 00:33:26,365
- Is it hot in here?
- No.

527
00:33:27,606 --> 00:33:30,474
- You all right?
- Yeah.

528
00:33:31,843 --> 00:33:33,869
You look a little pale.

529
00:33:34,246 --> 00:33:37,683
- Why are you perspiring so much?
- I'm just hot.

530
00:33:37,849 --> 00:33:40,148
It must be this costume.

531
00:33:40,619 --> 00:33:43,282
- Are you nervous, Dad?
- No.

532
00:33:44,556 --> 00:33:46,752
Oh, you are.

533
00:33:48,660 --> 00:33:51,186
Ben Matlock, the famous lawyer

534
00:33:51,363 --> 00:33:54,128
who thinks nothing of
giving press conferences

535
00:33:54,299 --> 00:33:57,963
that are seen by millions of
people on national television,

536
00:33:58,136 --> 00:34:02,870
is actually nervous about doing
some inconsequential little play.

537
00:34:04,376 --> 00:34:05,776
I'm not nervous.

538
00:34:05,944 --> 00:34:07,708
Wanna get some dinner?

539
00:34:07,879 --> 00:34:09,711
- No.
- Why not?

540
00:34:09,881 --> 00:34:12,009
I had a big lunch.

541
00:34:14,019 --> 00:34:16,511
If I eat anything
now, I'll throw up.

542
00:34:16,688 --> 00:34:19,783
Oh, Dad. Ha, ha.

543
00:34:19,958 --> 00:34:22,086
It's gonna be okay.

544
00:34:22,260 --> 00:34:25,253
Everybody gets nervous
before they go on. It's natural.

545
00:34:25,430 --> 00:34:28,923
Actually, it's good for you.
It keeps you on your toes.

546
00:34:30,435 --> 00:34:32,768
There are gonna
be critics out there.

547
00:34:32,938 --> 00:34:35,999
You're gonna be just fine.

548
00:34:37,475 --> 00:34:39,637
I'll tell Doug I can't do it.

549
00:34:39,811 --> 00:34:41,302
I'm too busy
preparing for his case.

550
00:34:41,479 --> 00:34:44,074
Like you said, a lot of
people don't have alibis.

551
00:34:44,249 --> 00:34:47,010
I got a lot of work to do. I'll just
tell him to put my understudy on.

552
00:34:47,152 --> 00:34:52,147
Dad, this isn't Broadway.
It's Willow Springs, Georgia.

553
00:34:52,757 --> 00:34:55,488
You don't have an understudy.

554
00:35:02,434 --> 00:35:04,665
It was just a thought.

555
00:35:14,779 --> 00:35:16,543
Bad news.

556
00:35:16,715 --> 00:35:18,775
That's what I have.

557
00:35:18,950 --> 00:35:22,443
I have bad news.

558
00:35:24,589 --> 00:35:27,388
There is no will.

559
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:34,524
Or if there is, no
one has found it yet.

560
00:35:35,300 --> 00:35:40,466
I called you here
for another reason,

561
00:35:40,639 --> 00:35:48,639
a little discussion
about murder.

562
00:35:49,381 --> 00:35:50,849
Marshall wasn't murdered.

563
00:35:51,016 --> 00:35:52,882
We all saw the body.

564
00:35:53,051 --> 00:35:56,783
He was ripped apart by
some hideous creature.

565
00:35:57,622 --> 00:35:59,022
The hounds.

566
00:35:59,190 --> 00:36:02,183
Oh, my God, it
must be the hounds.

567
00:36:02,360 --> 00:36:04,522
Superstitious rot.

568
00:36:04,996 --> 00:36:08,455
You wouldn't say that if you'd
seen what was left of him.

569
00:36:08,833 --> 00:36:10,893
It was...

570
00:36:17,308 --> 00:36:18,606
It was...

571
00:36:21,212 --> 00:36:23,807
Murder, Miss Isabel,
that's what it was.

572
00:36:23,982 --> 00:36:28,652
Oh, God, it was...

573
00:36:29,287 --> 00:36:30,687
We're dead.

574
00:36:34,959 --> 00:36:36,291
Huh?

575
00:36:36,461 --> 00:36:38,623
Murder, Miss Isabel,
that's what it was.

576
00:36:39,698 --> 00:36:44,534
And while I did
not find the will,

577
00:36:45,170 --> 00:36:47,833
I did find

578
00:36:56,314 --> 00:36:57,839
this.

579
00:37:04,389 --> 00:37:08,520
This is what killed your
stepfather, Miss Isabel,

580
00:37:08,693 --> 00:37:15,361
and the creature that wielded it
was all too human, I assure you.

581
00:37:15,533 --> 00:37:18,162
You mean you know
who killed Marshall?

582
00:37:18,336 --> 00:37:21,966
I prefer to let the facts
speak for themselves,

583
00:37:22,140 --> 00:37:25,702
and if you will come
with me to the library,

584
00:37:26,611 --> 00:37:31,379
they shall speak
volumes, I assure you.

585
00:37:50,401 --> 00:37:52,870
Ben, where are you going? Ben.

586
00:38:16,261 --> 00:38:18,821
Ben? Ben?

587
00:38:18,997 --> 00:38:22,263
Ben, get back up there.
You're in the next scene.

588
00:38:23,935 --> 00:38:27,167
We've got a show to
put on, Ben. Come on.

589
00:38:27,338 --> 00:38:29,136
Now.

590
00:38:39,784 --> 00:38:43,915
Ms. Dryden, I understand
the deceased, Glenda Yarnell,

591
00:38:44,088 --> 00:38:47,957
was your immediate superior
in the bank where you work.

592
00:38:48,126 --> 00:38:49,822
Yes, she was.

593
00:38:49,994 --> 00:38:52,225
And I hear that she demoted you

594
00:38:52,397 --> 00:38:56,892
from loan officer to bank
teller last January. Is that right?

595
00:38:57,068 --> 00:38:58,764
Well, it wasn't
really a demotion.

596
00:38:58,937 --> 00:39:02,999
Our bank had just merged, and
the new owners insisted on cutbacks.

597
00:39:03,174 --> 00:39:05,939
It was either accept a
lower-paying job or be let go.

598
00:39:06,110 --> 00:39:07,373
You sure you weren't demoted

599
00:39:07,545 --> 00:39:11,209
because you were accused
of misappropriating funds?

600
00:39:11,883 --> 00:39:13,351
How dare you?

601
00:39:13,518 --> 00:39:17,421
You know that accusation was
investigated and proven false.

602
00:39:18,656 --> 00:39:22,354
Who spearheaded
that investigation?

603
00:39:22,527 --> 00:39:24,792
I don't know.
Somebody from the bank.

604
00:39:24,963 --> 00:39:26,488
Was it the deceased?

605
00:39:26,931 --> 00:39:28,399
I said, I don't know.

606
00:39:28,566 --> 00:39:31,058
You mean the fact
that she demoted you

607
00:39:31,236 --> 00:39:35,139
one week after you were
accused is a coincidence?

608
00:39:35,306 --> 00:39:36,774
Yes.

609
00:39:36,941 --> 00:39:38,375
Was she punishing you?

610
00:39:39,344 --> 00:39:43,111
No. I just told you. I'd
done nothing wrong.

611
00:39:44,115 --> 00:39:46,550
You did a lot of
personal chores for her

612
00:39:46,718 --> 00:39:49,654
in the month before she
was murdered, didn't you?

613
00:39:49,821 --> 00:39:50,981
Well, what do you mean?

614
00:39:51,089 --> 00:39:54,582
Oh, I mean, things like taking
her car to the car wash at lunch

615
00:39:54,759 --> 00:39:56,455
and clothes to the dry cleaner's

616
00:39:56,628 --> 00:39:59,257
and buying her groceries
for her, you know.

617
00:39:59,430 --> 00:40:05,927
At least according to Mr. Selby,
Mrs. Gideon and Mr. Evans.

618
00:40:06,104 --> 00:40:07,197
That's what they tell me.

619
00:40:07,605 --> 00:40:12,339
They're ready to testify that you've
done business in their establishments

620
00:40:12,510 --> 00:40:16,038
since January on
behalf of Glenda Yarnell.

621
00:40:16,214 --> 00:40:21,448
Car wash, dry cleaner's,
groceries. Remember?

622
00:40:21,619 --> 00:40:25,488
I did favors for Glenda
from time to time, yes.

623
00:40:25,657 --> 00:40:30,595
According to them, you did
those favors practically every day.

624
00:40:30,762 --> 00:40:32,594
Were they favors?

625
00:40:32,764 --> 00:40:35,598
Or were they blackmail payments?

626
00:40:35,767 --> 00:40:37,099
They were favors.

627
00:40:37,869 --> 00:40:43,604
Glenda didn't force you to be
what amounted to her personal slave

628
00:40:43,775 --> 00:40:48,770
in return for keeping it a secret
that you were an embezzler?

629
00:40:48,947 --> 00:40:51,644
- No.
- You're reaching, Mr. Matlock.

630
00:40:51,816 --> 00:40:53,580
Your Honor, why would
she do all these things

631
00:40:53,751 --> 00:40:55,652
for somebody who
practically fired her?

632
00:40:55,820 --> 00:40:57,254
You're reaching, Mr. Matlock.

633
00:40:57,422 --> 00:40:59,687
- But, Your Honor. CLAGETT:
You're pressing your luck.

634
00:40:59,857 --> 00:41:03,316
Do you have to do any
typing as a bank teller?

635
00:41:04,228 --> 00:41:06,527
I use a keyboard to enter
information into the computer,

636
00:41:06,698 --> 00:41:08,064
if that's what you mean.

637
00:41:08,232 --> 00:41:10,792
Don't those long
fingernails get in your way?

638
00:41:12,403 --> 00:41:14,031
Not really.

639
00:41:14,205 --> 00:41:16,697
I noticed those fingernails

640
00:41:16,874 --> 00:41:20,333
opening night of the
play that we're both in.

641
00:41:20,511 --> 00:41:24,676
It's called <i>The Last
Cry of the Hounds.</i>

642
00:41:25,350 --> 00:41:30,084
I play Monroe
Angeline, the lead.

643
00:41:30,254 --> 00:41:32,086
Get on with it, Mr. Matlock.

644
00:41:33,324 --> 00:41:37,125
Are they your fingernails
or are they artificial?

645
00:41:37,295 --> 00:41:39,628
They're artificial. Acrylic.

646
00:41:39,797 --> 00:41:43,564
I noticed those fingernails
opening night, and it hit me,

647
00:41:43,735 --> 00:41:48,537
you weren't wearing long fingernails
during rehearsals, were you?

648
00:41:48,706 --> 00:41:51,733
No. I had them
put on for the play.

649
00:41:51,909 --> 00:41:56,313
It seemed to me that the character
I play would have long nails.

650
00:41:56,481 --> 00:41:58,575
Uh-huh. Or did you
have them put on

651
00:41:58,750 --> 00:42:01,447
to hide what happened
to your real nails?

652
00:42:01,619 --> 00:42:04,316
No. I was trying to
get into character,

653
00:42:04,489 --> 00:42:06,458
something you probably
wouldn't understand.

654
00:42:13,097 --> 00:42:16,090
The reason I mentioned the nails

655
00:42:16,701 --> 00:42:19,535
is the police just got
done taking apart the pipes

656
00:42:19,704 --> 00:42:23,072
under the sink in Glenda
Yarnell's dressing room.

657
00:42:23,241 --> 00:42:24,709
Do you know what they found?

658
00:42:26,044 --> 00:42:28,013
Two fingernails.

659
00:42:28,312 --> 00:42:29,541
Your fingernails.

660
00:42:29,714 --> 00:42:30,841
That is ridiculous.

661
00:42:31,015 --> 00:42:34,679
You tore them off during the struggle
with Glenda Yarnell, didn't you?

662
00:42:34,852 --> 00:42:36,480
Boy, that must have hurt.

663
00:42:36,654 --> 00:42:41,820
You ripped off two fingernails trying
to pull Doug's red tie around her neck,

664
00:42:41,993 --> 00:42:43,518
and she was
trying to pull it off.

665
00:42:43,694 --> 00:42:45,219
That's not true.

666
00:42:45,396 --> 00:42:47,196
The struggle took
place over the sink

667
00:42:47,331 --> 00:42:49,857
because that's where Glenda
put on her contact lenses.

668
00:42:50,034 --> 00:42:51,696
She put up a pretty good fight.

669
00:42:51,869 --> 00:42:52,996
But you won

670
00:42:53,171 --> 00:42:57,165
but ripped off two fingernails
in the process, whew,

671
00:42:57,341 --> 00:42:59,310
and then washed
them down the drain.

672
00:42:59,477 --> 00:43:00,672
No.

673
00:43:01,946 --> 00:43:04,381
I tore off a
fingernail one time.

674
00:43:04,549 --> 00:43:06,211
Boy, that hurt. Whew.

675
00:43:06,384 --> 00:43:08,979
- Did you ever tear off a fingernail?
- Mr. Matlock.

676
00:43:09,153 --> 00:43:14,353
And then, to make doubly sure
that no one would ever find them,

677
00:43:14,525 --> 00:43:18,519
you dragged the body to the middle
of the room, away from the sink,

678
00:43:18,696 --> 00:43:23,600
so that nobody would even be
tempted to look there. Is that about right?

679
00:43:23,768 --> 00:43:25,600
That is not true.

680
00:43:25,770 --> 00:43:27,136
Yes, it is.

681
00:43:27,305 --> 00:43:29,831
The lady who did your
nails, Mrs. McCowen,

682
00:43:31,375 --> 00:43:38,305
she's here, and she's ready to testify
that you had ripped off two fingernails,

683
00:43:38,483 --> 00:43:40,782
and your fingers were
bruised and discolored

684
00:43:40,952 --> 00:43:45,481
when you came in to have
those acrylic nails put on.

685
00:43:46,090 --> 00:43:49,891
And when the police run
a DNA test on those nails,

686
00:43:50,061 --> 00:43:55,466
whatever tissue they find on
them, I'm sure that'll about clinch it.

687
00:43:57,201 --> 00:44:01,468
Since the day Glenda caught
you with your hand in the till,

688
00:44:02,473 --> 00:44:04,840
you were her servant.

689
00:44:05,943 --> 00:44:07,741
She wasn't a nice woman.

690
00:44:07,912 --> 00:44:12,247
She probably was abusive to you.

691
00:44:12,984 --> 00:44:20,323
I guess that's the way you figured
the only way you could end it.

692
00:44:23,027 --> 00:44:26,930
Murder, Miss Isabel,
that's what it was.

693
00:44:39,477 --> 00:44:41,844
We find the
defendant not guilty.

694
00:44:42,613 --> 00:44:44,653
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen. Court is adjourned.

695
00:44:48,686 --> 00:44:51,281
Mm. BILLY: Ben, Ben!

696
00:44:51,455 --> 00:44:53,117
You were wonderful.
Masterful performance.

697
00:44:53,291 --> 00:44:57,285
Ben, you got reviewed
in this week's magazine.

698
00:44:57,461 --> 00:44:58,929
- What'd they say?
- Right there.

699
00:44:59,096 --> 00:45:01,292
You're a hit, Ben.
They loved you.

700
00:45:01,465 --> 00:45:03,297
"But the biggest surprise of all

701
00:45:03,467 --> 00:45:08,201
was the performance of real-life
attorney Benjamin Matlock,

702
00:45:09,207 --> 00:45:14,111
whose overwrought
delivery, robot-like gestures,

703
00:45:14,278 --> 00:45:19,012
and unremittingly
awkward stage presence

704
00:45:19,183 --> 00:45:22,779
almost single-handedly made
<i>The Last Cry of the Hounds</i>

705
00:45:22,954 --> 00:45:29,485
the best piece of camp theater
since Mel Brooks' <i>Springtime for Hitler."</i>

706
00:45:29,660 --> 00:45:32,186
See what I mean? You
were so bad you were good.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
